521
00:42:35,500 --> 00:42:38,500
Canapé is the brother of Inca.

522
00:42:38,500 --> 00:42:42,500
But while Inca is made from ITAS,

523
00:42:42,500 --> 00:42:49,500
and it's more used from a German manufacturer for the power chain side,

524
00:42:49,500 --> 00:42:55,500
Canapé is made by Vector, and it's used for almost every issue.

525
00:42:55,500 --> 00:43:04,500
So while engine developers are more confident with Inca,

526
00:43:04,500 --> 00:43:08,500
because the major part of the issues are made by Bosch,

527
00:43:08,500 --> 00:43:10,500
and Bosch made also the basic software,

528
00:43:10,500 --> 00:43:16,500
when they want a measurement, they want it in a format

529
00:43:16,500 --> 00:43:18,500
which can be compatible with Inca.

530
00:43:18,500 --> 00:43:23,500
So they want to adopt that file instead of an MDF or an MFO file.

531
00:43:23,500 --> 00:43:30,500
But yeah, we are going to use a lot of Canapé in validation activities,

532
00:43:30,500 --> 00:43:35,500
because it allows to do the same exact things of Inca,

533
00:43:35,500 --> 00:43:37,500
and it's maybe even easier to use,

534
00:43:37,500 --> 00:43:43,500
and more compatible with all other tools that we use in validation activities.

535
00:43:43,500 --> 00:43:46,500
With Cano, you can measure XEP.

536
00:43:46,500 --> 00:43:48,500
It's a little bit...

537
00:43:48,500 --> 00:43:53,500
Cano is not meant for doing calibration.

538
00:43:53,500 --> 00:43:59,500
It's more used to do simulations of a network and acquisition.

539
00:44:00,500 --> 00:44:07,500
Furthermore, Cano can also measure variables on internet, on LIM, and stuff like this.

540
00:44:07,500 --> 00:44:11,500
For now, we have only seen how to configure a Cannetwork,

541
00:44:11,500 --> 00:44:19,500
but it's possible to measure internet networks, LIM networks,

542
00:44:19,500 --> 00:44:25,500
flex-ray networks, and whatever networks you think about.

543
00:44:25,500 --> 00:44:31,500
And with specific licenses, it's possible even to access the standard decalibration protocol,

544
00:44:31,500 --> 00:44:34,500
